their UserPromptSubmit and PreToolUse to this hook when the mode is v2.

## Entry and last-good copy

`core/hook.py` is what every client runs; `core/runtime.py` is the logic (docs/adr/0028). The runtime
is edited in place, and on 2026-10-29 that put 32 hook errors into live sessions in 18 hours (a policy
key the hook and policy disagreed about, invalid JSON, a NameError, a SyntaxError), each failing open
silently. Now:

- When `runtime.py` cannot load or raises, `<state>/core/last-good/runtime.py` with its own
  `policy.json` answers, and the agent is told once per session and error (kept in
  `<state>/core/notices/`, apart from the session state). With no last-good copy the call fails open
  with that notice; a permission request is denied.
- Only tested bytes reach last-good: the `promote` row of `edit_checks` (core-v2) reads `runtime.py`
  and `policy.json` before `test_core_v2` and writes them after a pass only if they are unchanged
  (`LastGood` rows in `events.jsonl`). A session's first prompt starts the same test in the background,
  once per source version, when last-good does not hold the current source (a pull on another machine).
- Not covered: an error in `hook.py` itself (no fallback; `python-syntax` checks it at edit time) and the
  guard modules under `scripts/`, which load from the working tree and fail open one by one.
- A missing `hook.py` blocks every tool call (Python exits 2, a blocking hook error in Claude Code), so
  the file is replaced only by writing a temporary file and renaming it over the old one.

Loading the runtime as a module lets Python reuse its compiled bytecode instead of compiling the whole
file on every event: 5 to 7.5 ms less per event (paired runs below).

