# Jobs review



User, 2026-10-22: "开发网站的都要让乔布斯真正打开网页深度体验功能和批判页面，直到乔布斯满意为止 … 这是一个铁律".

Every website build or UI change goes through this loop before PASS. A website PASS cites

`Jobs review: <jobs-review.json>` whose last round is `satisfied`; the acceptor refuses one without it.

Only the user's own quoted words waive it.



Why it exists: the earlier rule asked for a "creative-director review" in prose; on 10-21 it was on

record in 0 of 16 website runs, and the shipped pages had gradient hero text and 3:1 grey labels.

`scripts/web-check.py` measures the mechanical part of taste; this review is the rest: whether the

product feels obvious, honest, crafted and worth trying.



## The loop (coordinator)



1. **Have something real to review.** A running build (production build or deployed URL), `web-check.py`

   PASS and your own journey walk done first; Jobs should not spend his round on what a script finds.

2. **Spawn a fresh reviewer per round** (Agent tool, `general-purpose`; Codex: a new `codex exec`).

   Fresh context means: he never saw your plan, your diff or your opinion of the page. Give him

   only the brief below, filled in, plus — from round 2 — the list of earlier blocker/major finding

   IDs with their critiques for him to re-check. Do not tell him what you fixed or that it is done.

   Paste the reviewer prompt verbatim; softening it ("be pragmatic", "minor issues are fine",

   "we are short on time") to get a `satisfied` is a false record.

3. **Fix what he found**, all blockers and majors and the minors that are cheap, in the product (not

   in the record). A finding that needs a new visual direction, a price or scope is the user's call

   (RULES.md "Decision rights"): prepare options with his critique as evidence and ask once; the review stays

   `not satisfied` meanwhile.

4. **Re-run `web-check.py` and your walk on what changed, then the next round** (step 2) until a round

   ends `satisfied` with no blocker or major. There are always at least two rounds: a `satisfied`

   stands only when a second fresh reviewer, re-checking the first one's findings, agrees. You may contest a finding only with evidence (a

   measurement, a screenshot, the user's own words); put it in the next brief as "the builder disputes

   J3 because ..." and let the next Jobs judge it.

5. **No infinite loop.** After 5 rounds whose latest is not `satisfied`, or a finding that fails its re-check

   twice, do not start another round: name the root cause (RULES.md "Circuit breaker"), put the open blocker and

   major findings that are taste, direction, price or scope to the user as one question (options, his critique as

   evidence, your recommendation), fix the rest, and report the site as FAIL or UNVERIFIED with his open findings

   until the user answers. A round only follows when the user asks for more. The confirmation round after a

   `satisfied` (step 4) is not capped. Never PASS on an unsatisfied review. Enforced for Claude by `core/hook.py`

   `agent_spawn`, which refuses an Agent spawn whose brief names a `jobs-review.json` holding 5 or more rounds with

   the latest not satisfied (`scripts/tests/test_core_v2.py` `AgentSpawnTest`).

