# Philosophy evidence and coverage — Codex review v2 r1

Read-only content audit, 2026-11-01. This is a specialist evidence ledger, not an acceptance verdict for the website.

Scope: `planning/BRIEF-v2.md`, all 44 entries in `src/content/sources.ts` (quotation, reference, gloss and limits), all 23 session introductions and all 119 authored rounds/levels in the seven game content files. An independent local evaluation of the TypeScript content exports counted the entries; it did not exercise gameplay. UI behaviour, image interpretation and persistence are the coordinator's separate evidence.

The initial source read preceded another actor's changes to `cafe/rounds.ts`; the exported `authored-data.json` snapshot used below contains the later café notes, including c4-4's PI §43 note. No source was edited by this reviewer. The coordinator should match these to its production journey JSON, since an existing production bundle need not match the working tree.

## Primary editions consulted

- [PI: G. E. M. Anscombe, Blackwell English text, third edition/reprint](https://static1.squarespace.com/static/54889e73e4b0a2c1f9891289/t/564b61a4e4b04eca59c4d232/1447780772744/Ludwig.Wittgenstein.-.Philosophical.Investigations.pdf): title and copyright pages identify translator/edition. PDF text extraction was compared with the source catalog, with ellipses, punctuation and OCR artifacts examined separately.
- [OC: Denis Paul and G. E. M. Anscombe, Blackwell 1969–1975 text](https://prawfsblawg.blogs.com/files/wittgenstein-on-certainty.pdf); [scanned edition](https://circuitdebater.org/w/archive_files/On%20Certainty.pdf/507953768/On%20Certainty.pdf) independently supplies §96 where the first PDF's web extraction omits a table cell. Downloads of both OC hosts returned HTTP 403; the web tool successfully read their indexed PDFs. This did not prevent section checking.
- [TLP: 1922 Ogden edition, Wittgenstein Project/Gutenberg transcription](https://wittgensteinproject.org/w/index.php/Tractatus_Logico-Philosophicus_(English)), whose edition note identifies the 1922 source.

## Actionable findings

### PH-1 — major — The Pupil still sets English proficiency as its task

Location: `/pupil`, all three sessions, levels `p-1`–`p-8`; `src/games/pupil/levels.ts:8`, `:44`, `:72`, `:113`, `:156`, `:196`, `:242`, `:277`; `planning/BRIEF-v2.md:8`.

The tasks explicitly ask players to teach articles, plurals, past spelling, comparatives, verb complementation, countability, third-person endings and -ing spelling. The exam and twist answers are English forms, including `a historic`, `children`, `travelled`, `cleverer`, `stop smoking`, `the data is`, `she has`, and `seeing`. The philosophical end notes do not change what success requires. This directly conflicts with the brief's explicit exclusion of English as a learning goal or scored item.

Concrete fix: retain the train/test/interpretation mechanism but replace grammar mastery with directly visible rule-following cases (for example, continuations of a symbol/number sequence, rival continuations after a boundary, and shared correction practices). Have the player distinguish a rule's expression, an interpretation and its application; explain why the finite toy model stops short of PI §§185–202. The brief, rather than a disputed interpretation, decides this finding.

### PH-2 — major — Truth conditions are confused with a unique arrangement

Location: `/scene`, `scene-3`, `s3-1`–`s3-6`; `src/games/scene/rounds.ts:168`.

The session claims: “A proposition fixes reality to a yes or a no: one sentence has to leave one possibility (Tractatus 4.023).” The second clause is not a consequence of 4.023. Determinate truth conditions can admit several arrangements: 4.2 and 4.431 explicitly concern agreement/disagreement with possibilities. The game's own `s1-3` allows the lamp anywhere left of the cup.

Concrete fix: say “A proposition has determinate truth conditions. In this communication puzzle, your partner additionally needs enough information to choose one position.” Label uniqueness as the puzzle's extra constraint and cite 4.023–4.024/4.2. [Primary text](https://wittgensteinproject.org/w/index.php/Tractatus_Logico-Philosophicus_(English)#4.023).

### PH-3 — major — Doubt is incorrectly said to presuppose the disputed object's existence

Location: `/hinges`, `hinges-1`, `h1-4`, wrong-answer feedback; `src/games/hinges/rounds.ts:79`.

The feedback requires trusting “that the things she doubts about exist”. Ordinary existence questions are counterexamples. OC §20 expressly distinguishes doubting the external world from doubting a planet's existence. §115 requires some certainty, not certainty of the proposition under dispute.

Concrete fix: replace that clause with “some uses of words and ways of checking must already stand fast”. [Primary text, §§20 and 115](https://prawfsblawg.blogs.com/files/wittgenstein-on-certainty.pdf).

### PH-4 — minor — The first beetle round omits a premise needed by its answer key

Location: `/beetle`, `beetle-1`, `bb1-1`; `src/games/beetle/rounds.ts:17`, `:28`, `:31`.

The setup only forbids looking into another box, but marks asking for a description wrong and says nothing could settle relative size. The setup leaves shared measuring/reporting procedures open. The stronger own-case restriction appears only later in `bb1-3`.

Concrete fix: introduce the full stipulated privacy condition before the first question: there is no shared procedure for comparing the contents, and each speaker claims to learn the term solely from their own case. Make the consequence conditional on that premise. [PI §293](https://static1.squarespace.com/static/54889e73e4b0a2c1f9891289/t/564b61a4e4b04eca59c4d232/1447780772744/Ludwig.Wittgenstein.-.Philosophical.Investigations.pdf).

### PH-5 — minor — English quotations link only to German source pages

Location: `/sources`, source links for PI and OC; `src/content/sources.ts:20`, `:22`.

The links open the German texts, not the named English translations. This prevents an English-speaking beginner from checking the quotation against the linked edition.

Concrete fix: provide an English edition link/bibliographic entry with section/page guidance; retain the existing links explicitly labelled “German original”. Both destinations were opened and their language/edition confirmed: [PI German](https://www.wittgensteinproject.org/w/index.php/Philosophische_Untersuchungen), [OC German](https://www.wittgensteinproject.org/w/index.php/%C3%9Cber_Gewi%C3%9Fheit).

### PH-6 — minor — One quotation silently modernizes the checked translation

Location: `/sources`, `pi-270`; `src/content/sources.ts:370`.

The catalog has `shows`; the checked Anscombe edition prints `shews` (printed p. 95e, PDF page 52). The meaning is unchanged. This is the only lexical quotation discrepancy found; punctuation, capitalization at extracted sentence starts, curly quotation marks and explicit ellipses are not treated as substantive errors.

Concrete fix: restore `shews`, or state that spelling is modernized and identify the exact edition used. [PI §270](https://static1.squarespace.com/static/54889e73e4b0a2c1f9891289/t/564b61a4e4b04eca59c4d232/1447780772744/Ludwig.Wittgenstein.-.Philosophical.Investigations.pdf#page=52).

### PH-7 — minor — Demonstrative reference is described as teaching a new name

Location: `/builder`, `builder-3`, `b3-5`–`b3-6`; `src/content/sources.ts:48` and `src/games/builder/rounds.ts:167`.

The §6 gloss says pointing and saying `this` grows the vocabulary. It conflates using a demonstrative with teaching `arch` as a new word. The round silently adds that name after success.

Concrete fix: show an explicit naming/training step for `arch`; distinguish it from the already-understood demonstrative `this` (§8). [PI §§6–9, 27–30](https://static1.squarespace.com/static/54889e73e4b0a2c1f9891289/t/564b61a4e4b04eca59c4d232/1447780772744/Ludwig.Wittgenstein.-.Philosophical.Investigations.pdf).

